"plookup: A simplified polynomial protocol for lookup tables (https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/315)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/315\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 70\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2020/315\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†plookup: A simplified polynomial protocol for lookup tables】\nL32: \nL33: Ariel Gabizon and Zachary J. Williamson\nL34: ##### Abstract\nL35: We present a protocol for checking the values of a committed polynomial $f\\in \\mathbb{F}_{<n}[X]$ over a multiplicative subgroup $H\\subset \\mathbb{F}$ of size $n$, are contained in the values of a table $t\\in \\mathbb{F}^d$. Our protocol can be viewed as a simplification of one from Bootle et. al [BCGJM, ASIACRYPT 2018] for a similar problem, with potential efficiency improvements when $d\\leq n$.\nL36: In particular, [BCGJM]'s protocol requires comitting to several auxiliary polynomials of degree $d\\cdot \\log n$, whereas ours requires three commitments to auxiliary polynomials of degree $n$, which can be much smaller in the case $d\\sim n$. One common use case of this primitive in the zk-SNARK setting is a ``batched range proof'', where one wishes to check all of $f$'s values on $H$ are in a range $[0,\\ldots,M]$.\nL37: We present a slightly optimized protocol for this special case, and pose improving it as an open problem.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkllm-ccs2024/LICENSE at main · jvhs0706/zkllm-ccs2024 · GitHub (https://github.com/jvhs0706/zkllm-ccs2024/blob/main/LICENSE)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/jvhs0706/zkllm-ccs2024/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 264\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your code as you build】\nL23:       * 【12†Secret protectionStop leaks before they start】\nL24: \nL25:     * EXPLORE\nL26:       * 【13†Why GitHub】\nL27:       * 【14†Documentation†docs.github.com】\nL28:       * 【15†Blog†github.blog】\nL29:       * 【16†Changelog†github.blog】\nL30:       * 【17†Marketplace】\nL31: \nL32: 【18†View all features】\nL33: \nL34:   * Solutions\nL35:     * BY COMPANY SIZE\nL36:       * 【19†Enterprises】\nL37:       * 【20†Small and medium teams】\nL38:       * 【21†Startups】\nL39:       * 【22†Nonprofits】\nL40: \nL41:     * BY USE CASE\nL42:       * 【23†App Modernization】\nL43:       * 【24†DevSecOps】\nL44:       * 【25†DevOps】\nL45:       * 【26†CI/CD】\nL46:       * 【27†View all use cases】\nL47: \nL48:     * BY INDUSTRY\nL49:       * 【28†Healthcare】\nL50:       * 【29†Financial services】\nL51:       * 【30†Manufacturing】\nL52:       * 【31†Government】\nL53:       * 【32†View all industries】\nL54: \nL55: 【33†View all solutions】\nL56: \nL57:   * Resources\nL58:     * EXPLORE BY TOPIC\nL59:       * 【34†AI】\nL60:       * 【35†Software Development】\nL61:       * 【36†DevOps】\nL62:       * 【37†Security】\nL63:       * 【38†View all topics】\nL64: \nL65:     * EXPLORE BY TYPE\nL66:       * 【39†Customer stories】\nL67:       * 【40†Events & webinars】\nL68:       * 【41†Ebooks & reports】\nL69:       * 【42†Business insights】\nL70:       * 【43†GitHub Skills†skills.github.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.08258v2)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"pattern\":\"RTX\"}); Total lines: 437\nNo matching text found for \"RTX\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.08258v2)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"pattern\":\"implementation\"}); Total lines: 437\nL183: Baselines. We compare against prior zero-knowledge proofs of training (zkPoT) and confidential DP-SGD certification. Garg et al.’s logistic-regression zkPoT (【44†Garg et al., 2023】) is the only one of these with a public implementation, and we re-execute it on our own hardware. Public implementations of Kaizen, ZKAudit, and Confidential-DPproof (【43†Shamsabadi et al., 2024】) are unavailable, so we use the costs reported in the corresponding papers and mark them as estimates.\nL184: Hardware and software. All operator measurements used a dedicated NVIDIA A100-PCIE (40 GB) server with an Intel Xeon CPU (80 threads), 629 GB of RAM, Ubuntu 22.04, and CUDA 12.4. The mcl verifier ran on the same server’s CPU without using the GPU. The GPU was not shared during the reported measurements. Contention can dominate protocol time, and we observed slowdowns of up to $70{\\times}$ on the same configuration when other tenants were active.\nL185: Metrics. For each configuration we report the proving time, verification time, and proof size of the covered operators. The proving times are aggregated from per-operator benchmarks measured on the A100, as described above. We report commitment time separately from proving time. The commitment to released weights is paid once when the model is published and can be reused across later audits, while commitments to activations are generated for each audit.\nL186: ### 4.2 Implementation\nL187: The prototype contains a CUDA/C++ prover and a separate C++ verifier. The prover reuses the BLS12-381 field and curve kernels of zkLLM (【40†Sun et al., 2024】) for GPU arithmetic. On top of them, we implement Pedersen commitments in a Hyrax-style layout, sumcheck arguments for linear operators, and a logup-style lookup argument for nonlinear functions. The verifier is a separate C++ binary that links mcl for BLS12-381 arithmetic and contains no CUDA.\nL188: The protocol supports cross-entropy and custom terminal objectives, and the fairness, membership, and safety audits reuse the same circuit through the objective interface. Model dimensions and operator counts come from the public configurations, while benchmark inputs are synthetic fixed-point integers.\nL189: ### 4.3 Main Results\nL190: Table 【60†1】 compares zkLLMPoT with prior training-oriented ZK systems under each paper’s own model and dataset. Each row names the system and the model it was evaluated on, then reports the parameter count and four costs. Commit is the time to bind the released weights; when a cell says “in prove”, that cost is folded into proving. Prove is the time to produce the certificate, Verify is the auditor’s checking time, and Proof is the size of the transferable object.\nL191: Kaizen and ZKAudit quote these costs per training iteration, so a fine-tuning run multiplies them by the step count: Kaizen needs $882$ s per iteration on VGG-11, and DPproof reports $100$ hours for DP-SGD on CIFAR-10 features. DPproof is interactive, so its auditor stays online throughout and produces no proof object.\nL192: The zkLLMPoT rows are one-shot results at $T{=}512$ on $1.1$–$1.5$B checkpoints: commitment is $138$–$206$ s, proving $41$–$59$ s, verification below half a second, and proofs about $17$–$20$ MiB. The relation contains a single forward evaluation, so the cost does not grow with the number of training steps, and the evaluated checkpoints have two orders of magnitude more parameters than the largest prior system in the table.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.08258v2)\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"pattern\":\"131\"}); Total lines: 437\nL48: We build on sumcheck- and lookup-based arguments to certify Transformer computations, while supporting next-token loss and task-specific audit objectives. Across four model families, operator-level benchmarks yield proving times of 41–59 seconds for 1.1–1.5B-parameter models and 131 seconds at 13B for the covered operators, with verification below half a second at a sequence length of 512.\nL49: ## 1 Introduction\nL50: Large language models (LLMs) increasingly serve as critical infrastructure for language understanding, generation, and decision support (【31†Brown et al., 2020】), yet assessing their training outcomes raises pressing privacy and trustworthiness concerns.\nL51: Trained models can memorize and leak sensitive examples (【32†Shokri et al., 2017】; 【33†Carlini et al., 2021】), so institutions may withhold training data and model weights from external auditors, leaving the public with only a model card or an API endpoint.\nL63: To make the proof practical, zkLLMPoT evaluates the committed checkpoint through a loss-function-style objective. This is a feasible measure of training outcomes, because the loss a checkpoint attains on held-out data from the target distribution is the standard way to judge whether pretraining or fine-tuning achieved its goal (【46†Gururangan et al., 2020】). To avoid the trainer overfitting the evaluation, we specified a data commitment protocol.\nL64: The auditor specifies the objective, the trainer freezes the corresponding circuit and commits the checkpoint, and the auditor then chooses the evaluation sequences. The trainer is not able to modify her weights after seeing the commitment.\nL65: Together, these techniques let zkLLMPoT generate and verify proofs quickly, with proving times of $41$–$59$ s for the covered operators on $1.1$–$1.5$B-parameter models and $131$ s at $13$B, and verification below half a second at sequence length $512$, while the commit-then-challenge order removes the trainer’s opportunity to overfit the checkpoint to the auditor’s sequences.\nL66: In summary, our contributions are:\nL67: \nL68:   * •\nL69: \nL70: An outcome-attestation formulation that audits properties attributed to LLM training through a loss-function-style evaluation of committed weights, excluding the backward pass and optimizer from the proved relation;\nL71: \nL72:   * •\nL73: \nL74: A commit-then-challenge protocol in which the trainer binds the circuit and checkpoint before the auditor selects the evaluation sequences, which then enter the statement as public inputs;\nL75: \nL76:   * •\nL77: Operator-level optimizations and fusion for compiling Transformer layers into compact ZK circuits, plus an interface for auditor-specified terminal objectives;\nL78: \nL79:   * •\nL80: \nL81: A component-based study across four model families, yielding proving times of $41$–$59$ s for the covered operators at the $1.x$B scale and $131$ s at $13$B, with verification below half a second.\nL82: ## 2 Preliminaries\nL83: \nL84: In this section, we introduce the properties a zero-knowledge protocol is required to satisfy, and the threat model of our method. We first recall completeness, soundness, zero knowledge, and succinctness for the evaluation relation in section 【6†2.1】. We then state the two-party threat model in section 【7†2.2】.\nL85: ### 2.1 Zero Knowledge Proof\nL86: A zero-knowledge succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge (zk-SNARK) lets a prover convince an auditor that it knows a witness $w$ for a public statement $x$ of an NP relation $R$, without revealing $w$ and without the auditor re-executing the computation (【47†Goldwasser et al., 1989】; 【48†Bitansky et al., 2012】; 【49†Groth, 2016】). Here $R$ is a set of pairs $(x,w)$, and its associated language $L_{R}=\\{\\,x\\mid\\exists\\,w:(x,w)\\in R\\,\\}$ is the set of statements that admit a witness.\nL87: We instantiate $R$ with the definition of model training: the public input is the declared architecture $\\mathcal{A}$, the objective, the evaluation sequences $S$, a commitment to the released weights $\\theta$, and a claimed scalar $v$; the witness is $\\theta$ and the intermediate activations, with a reference checkpoint added when the statement compares two model states.\nL88: An accepting proof convinces the auditor that $v$ is the value the stated objective, such as the output of a loss function, takes on $f_{\\mathcal{A},\\theta}(S)$. We require the following properties for the evaluation relation above.\nL89:   * •\nL90: \nL91: Completeness. If the committed weights, public sequences, activations, and objective value satisfy the declared forward relation, then an honest prover can produce a proof that an honest auditor accepts.\nL92: \nL93:   * •\nL94: Soundness. For every (efficient) malicious prover $\\mathcal{P}^{*}$ and every statement $x\\notin L_{R}$, the verifier accepts $\\mathcal{P}^{*}$’s proof with only negligible probability, which means a trainer cannot substitute uncommitted weights, change the declared architecture or objective, or claim a value $v$ that the committed $\\theta$ does not attain on the public $S$.\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL218: ## 5 Conclusion\nL219: \nL220: Without a certificate bound to the released checkpoint, a trainer can advertise domain adaptation, instruction tuning, or a safety and fairness property that the model does not attain, or substitute a weaker checkpoint and still pass an evaluation on a known test set. The harm is an accepted false claim about the checkpoint.\nL221: We presented zkLLMPoT, a zero-knowledge framework for auditing outcomes attributed to LLM training without proving the full training trajectory. By committing the model before the auditor selects challenge sequences, zkLLMPoT reduces the certified computation to a forward evaluation under an auditor-specified objective, making the proof cost independent of the number of training iterations.\nL222: Across four LLM families, the proving time is 41–59 seconds at the 1.xB scale and 131 seconds at 13B parameters, with verification below half a second. These results suggest a practical path toward succinct and privacy-preserving auditing of trained LLM checkpoints.\nL223: We discuss several future directions: (1) faster operator designs and tighter fusion that extend the same one-shot relation to larger models and longer sequences; (2) more flexible user-defined audits of training outcomes, beyond a fixed catalog of loss, fairness, membership, and safety objectives, while still compiling to the same committed forward circuit; and (3) protocol designs that admit multiple trainers, auditors, or downstream verifiers when several parties jointly hold a checkpoint or jointly set the challenge, without revealing $\\theta$ or the private corpus.\nL224: ## AI use statement\nL225: We used generative AI tools to polish the writing, to help check experimental outputs, and to organize recorded measurements into tables and figures. We did not use them to generate research ideas, to design the protocol or its gadgets, to formulate or prove claims, or to produce any reported number: every measurement comes from running our own code on hardware we control. All AI-assisted edits were reviewed by the authors before inclusion, and the use of these tools remained under our direction.\nL226: We take responsibility for the final content of this work, including text, claims, and artifacts produced with the aid of generative AI.\nL227: ## Ethics statement\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nperf: booleanity memory optimization [JOLT-206] by 0xAndoroid · Pull Request #936 · a16z/jolt · GitHub (https://github.com/a16z/jolt/pull/936)\nciteturn4view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view4\",\"pattern\":\"merged\"}); Total lines: 322\nL150: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL151: ### Uh oh!\nL152: \nL153: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL154: \nL155: 【63†a16z】 / 【64†jolt】 Public\nL156: \nL157:   * 【65†Notifications】 You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL158:   * 【65†Fork 324】\nL159:   * 【65†Star】\nL160: \nL161:   * 【64†Code】\nL162:   * 【66†Issues 15】\nL163:   * 【67†Pull requests 29】\nL164:   * 【68†Actions】\nL165:   * 【69†Projects】\nL166:   * 【70†Security and quality 0】\nL167:   * 【71†Insights】\nL168: \nL169: Additional navigation options\nL170: # perf: booleanity memory op